# Owners are set on what cannot be undone, not on everything.
#
# There used to be a `* @FHIDev/fhi-munin-maintainer` line here, which requested the team's
# review on every pull request — a docs typo and a change to the publish pipeline arrived
# looking exactly alike. An automatic request that lands on everything is one people learn to
# dismiss, and dismissing it is a habit that does not switch off for the pull request where it
# mattered. So the wildcard is gone and only the irreversible paths keep an owner.
#
# The test for whether something belongs below: can a mistake here be fixed by a follow-up
# commit? If yes, leave it out. Ordinary component and sample work is recoverable and is not
# listed. What is listed is rarely touched, so a request from this file should be rare enough
# to still mean something.
#
# Team ownership rather than an individual's, so a package other services depend on does not
# hang off one person's account.

# Publishing is the irreversible one: a version pushed to nuget.org can be unlisted, never
# withdrawn. That covers the workflow that publishes, the scripts that guard what goes into
# the package, and the version and metadata the package is stamped with.
/.github/workflows/       @FHIDev/fhi-munin-maintainer
/scripts/                 @FHIDev/fhi-munin-maintainer
/Directory.Build.props    @FHIDev/fhi-munin-maintainer

# The build error that keeps host-specific APIs out of a library that has to render inside
# helsedata's legacy Blazor Server. Weakening it does not fail anything here; it fails later,
# in someone else's site.
/BannedSymbols.txt        @FHIDev/fhi-munin-maintainer

# This file, so ownership cannot be quietly removed by the same pull request that needs it.
/.github/CODEOWNERS       @FHIDev/fhi-munin-maintainer
